Part 1 — What is Recursion?

      Suppose the interviewer asks:
          "What is recursion, and when would you choose recursion over an iterative solution?"
      
      Don't give me a textbook definition.
          Answer as if you're explaining your design choice to a Google DeepMind L5 interviewer.
      
      Think about:      
          What happens to the problem at every recursive call?
          What makes recursion eventually terminate?
          What is the relationship between the original problem and the smaller problem?
          When is recursion particularly useful?

Question 1
  Explain recursion in your own words and give me one example where recursion naturally fits the problem.


1. Recursion is not necessarily "the same computation"
  A recursive function doesn't always perform exactly the same computation. More precisely:
  
                A recursive solution solves a problem by reducing it 
                into one or more smaller instances of the same problem.

    The problem definition remains the same, but the input/state becomes smaller.
================================================================================================================================================
The better distinction is between Iteration and recusrion:

"I choose recursion when the problem has a natural recursive structure 
  and expressing the solution recursively makes the state transitions clearer. 
  I choose iteration when recursion doesn't provide enough benefit or when 
  I want to avoid call-stack overhead."


⭐ L5 Interview Answer

You could say:

    "Recursion is a technique 
      where a function 
          solves a problem by 
            reducing it into smaller instances 
            of the same problem 
            and calling itself on those smaller instances. 

      A recursive solution 
        requires a 
          1. base case 
              that terminates the recursion 
          and 
          2. a recursive case that makes 
              measurable progress toward that base case.

    I would consider 
          recursion 
              when the problem naturally has a recursive structure
                  —for example, 
                        tree traversal, 
                        divide-and-conquer algorithms, 
                      or generating subsets and permutations. 


            I would prefer iteration 
                when the recursive structure doesn't add clarity 
                or when recursion depth could create stack-overflow concerns."


